1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,585
Mandate Wallet lets an AI agent purchase an approved API service
within an owner-defined budget.

2
00:00:06,585 --> 00:00:14,049
We will connect it to Claude Desktop, obtain an ETH statistics sample,
and produce a short brief.

3
00:00:14,049 --> 00:00:18,000
The transactions in this demonstration run on Base Sepolia.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Prepare Claude Desktop, Rabby and Node twenty-four, then start Mandate
using the manual’s setup command.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The owner needs test ETH and USDC. This demo uses controlled services
and fixed statistics.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:43,412
Choose Testnet and Base Sepolia in the setup wizard. Connect the owner
wallet, then review deployment,

7
00:00:43,412 --> 00:00:55,176
funding and permissions. We transfer zero point one five test USDC to
the smart account and fund operator gas separately.

8
00:00:55,176 --> 00:01:04,588
The task budget is zero point one five, each payment is capped at zero
point one,

9
00:01:04,588 --> 00:01:14,000
and the session expires after two hours. The owner confirms these
transactions in the external wallet.

10
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:21,579
After confirmation, open the authorization transaction in BaseScan.
Check the Base Sepolia network, transaction hash and successful
receipt.

11
00:01:21,579 --> 00:01:30,000
The contract log links the session to the approved mandate. The
complete hash will also appear in our execution evidence.

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,652
Download the Guided package for this Base Sepolia configuration. In
Claude Desktop, open Settings,

13
00:01:40,652 --> 00:01:52,826
Extensions and Advanced settings, then import and enable the package.
Its local connection is already configured,

14
00:01:52,826 --> 00:02:05,000
so no Agent profile or Connection file is selected. Start a new
conversation using this connection.

15
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,857
Ask Claude to read the account context and quote the approved ETH
sample without purchasing.

16
00:02:12,857 --> 00:02:22,286
Check the chain, account, active session and remaining budget. The
sample costs zero point zero one test USDC.

17
00:02:22,286 --> 00:02:27,000
Installing the connector does not expand the approved authority.

18
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:40,750
Authorize the quoted purchase. Mandate registers the commitment before
releasing a payment signature. The USDC transfer settles on Base
Sepolia,

19
00:02:40,750 --> 00:02:52,438
but our controlled service deliberately fails its first response.
Claude reports payment, delivery and result validation separately.

20
00:02:52,438 --> 00:03:00,000
A successful payment alone does not mean the data was delivered.

21
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:09,565
Open the settlement transaction in BaseScan. Check Success, the test
USDC contract, and the ERC-twenty transfer from the smart account

22
00:03:09,565 --> 00:03:18,652
to the approved recipient for zero point zero one USDC. The
transaction sender may be the facilitator paying gas;

23
00:03:18,652 --> 00:03:22,000
the token-transfer sender identifies the paying account.

24
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:32,082
Claude resumes the same ETH step after checking its payment state. The
original order and settlement hash are retained,

25
00:03:32,082 --> 00:03:41,102
and there is no second payment. The service returns the sample and the
result checker validates it.

26
00:03:41,102 --> 00:03:48,000
Claude writes a brief, identifies the fixed demonstration dataset, and
cites the settlement.

27
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:57,154
Claude prepares a revocation request. The owner reviews it in the
wallet workspace and signs in Rabby.

28
00:03:57,154 --> 00:04:07,923
The BaseScan receipt and SessionRevoked log confirm the change. Future
spending by this session is blocked while the completed purchase

29
00:04:07,923 --> 00:04:09,000
remains recorded.

30
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:18,273
Finally, ask Claude to attempt the unused BTC sample. The revoked
session prevents a new payment registration.

31
00:04:18,273 --> 00:04:28,091
This refusal has no new transaction hash because it is stopped before
broadcast. We retain the tool result,

32
00:04:28,091 --> 00:04:33,000
permission state and unchanged payment records as refusal evidence.

33
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:40,083
The evidence package connects each recorded step to its transaction,
contract events and service outcome.

34
00:04:40,083 --> 00:04:49,528
Payment and service delivery are documented separately. A local
practice option is also available for learning the workflow without
public-testnet

35
00:04:49,528 --> 00:04:50,000
funds.
